WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.590
A timetable can be drawn as a network.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.060
Each person is a dot, and each meeting that must happen

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.790
is a line between two dots.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.490
Meetings in the same time slot must not share a person.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.950
So a time slot is a set of lines with no dot in common.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.000
Drawing it this way turns "avoid clashes" into something

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:19.730
you can see.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.840
It also gives quick lower limits on the number of slots,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.690
before you build anything.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.990
Four players each play one game against

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.180
every other player.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.420
A player can play at most one game per round.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.180
What is the smallest number of rounds needed?

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.880
Pause and count the games.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.890
There are six games.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.000
A round uses each player at most once, so it holds at

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.010
most two games.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.550
Six divided by two is three.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.690
At least three rounds are needed.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:53.850
Can three be reached?

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.890
Round one: A plays B, and C plays D.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.210
Round two: A plays C, and B plays D.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.170
Round three: A plays D, and B plays C.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.890
All six games are played.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.510
Three rounds is the answer.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.370
With five players there are ten games.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.040
In any round one player must rest, so again at most

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.090
two games fit.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.420
That takes at least five rounds, even though each player

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.910
plays only four games.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.160
The five-day pairing schedule from the earlier lesson

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.270
shows that five rounds can be reached.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.880
There is a second kind of limit.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:31.030
A person who is in three meetings needs at least three

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.340
slots, whatever everyone else is doing.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.730
Use whichever limit is stronger, and then show a

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.220
timetable that reaches it.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.480
Draw people as dots and meetings as lines.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.380
A slot is a set of lines sharing no dot.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.920
Find a limit, then build a timetable that reaches it.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.530
Notebook challenge: six players each play every other

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.970
player once, with at most three games per round and no

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.620
player playing twice in a round.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:58.420
What is the fewest rounds?
